WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.459 --> 00:00:05.794
Kat Craig: Tamana, welcome.

00:00:05.835 --> 00:00:07.796
It's so wonderful to have you here today.

00:00:07.837 --> 00:00:09.798
Thank you for joining us.

00:00:09.839 --> 00:00:13.510
We're going to be talking
about your directorial feature debut

00:00:13.552 --> 00:00:14.803
"In Her Hands."

00:00:14.844 --> 00:00:17.931
I can't wait to hear
all about that process

00:00:17.973 --> 00:00:20.767
of making such a beautiful film.

00:00:20.809 --> 00:00:23.103
But before we start,
can you tell us a little bit

00:00:23.144 --> 00:00:25.981
about what it was like
when you were younger in Afghanistan,

00:00:25.981 --> 00:00:27.566
what it was like growing up?

00:00:27.566 --> 00:00:31.111
Tamana Ayazi: Thank you so much.
It's lovely to be here with you.

00:00:31.111 --> 00:00:33.780
Some of the good memories
I have from my childhood

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:38.493
[are] being able to play soccer
on the street with boys, with my friends.

00:00:38.493 --> 00:00:40.412
And also I used to have a bike.

00:00:40.412 --> 00:00:46.042
I used to go out every evening
with my friends

00:00:46.084 --> 00:00:47.752
to bring groceries,

00:00:47.794 --> 00:00:51.256
to just go, you know,
spend some time together

00:00:51.298 --> 00:00:53.717
and just be there and exist.

00:00:54.551 --> 00:00:58.138
KC: And that very active life
has continued for you.

00:00:58.179 --> 00:00:59.931
I know that you started making films

00:00:59.931 --> 00:01:01.182
at a really young age,

00:01:01.224 --> 00:01:03.602
when you were still
in high school, is that right?

00:01:03.643 --> 00:01:06.229
TA: Yes, I was in 11th grade
when I started.

00:01:06.229 --> 00:01:08.148
I was part of a program

00:01:08.148 --> 00:01:11.026
called Citizen Journalism
and Documentary Filmmaking,

00:01:11.026 --> 00:01:15.488
and I was representing my hometown,
Mazar, north of Afghanistan.

00:01:15.488 --> 00:01:18.325
And that’s how I started making films.

00:01:18.325 --> 00:01:21.161
And it was -- we were supposed
to make one documentary,

00:01:21.161 --> 00:01:26.458
one short documentary per month
from pre-production to post-production.

00:01:26.499 --> 00:01:28.460
And we were very excited about that.

00:01:28.501 --> 00:01:30.503
And it opened other doors.

00:01:30.545 --> 00:01:33.798
I was able to go to the places
I have never been

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:36.092
and I was able
to meet people, communicate,

00:01:36.134 --> 00:01:39.596
listen to them
and try to tell their stories.

00:01:40.138 --> 00:01:42.932
KC: Let's talk a little bit
about "In Her Hands."

00:01:42.974 --> 00:01:46.436
Can you summarize a little bit
about what the film is about

00:01:46.478 --> 00:01:49.189
and why you chose to tell that story?

00:01:49.189 --> 00:01:51.399
TA: The film is about a strong woman

00:01:51.399 --> 00:01:54.527
who was trying to make a change
in her community.

00:01:54.527 --> 00:01:58.156
And like many other Afghans
from my generation,

00:01:58.156 --> 00:02:02.494
Zarifa Ghafari was trying
to make a change in her own hometown

00:02:02.494 --> 00:02:04.954
of Maidan Shar, Maidan Wardak.

00:02:04.954 --> 00:02:07.749
And she was quite famous in Afghanistan,

00:02:07.791 --> 00:02:10.627
so I was really curious
to know about her more.

00:02:10.669 --> 00:02:12.712
So that's how I visited her.

00:02:12.754 --> 00:02:18.176
And then we were trying to make a film
not only about who Zarifa was,

00:02:18.218 --> 00:02:20.512
but also the country itself.

00:02:20.553 --> 00:02:24.974
So we tried to include other characters
to be able to tell a story of the country,

00:02:25.016 --> 00:02:26.518
not only one person.

00:02:27.477 --> 00:02:29.521
And the journey of the country

00:02:29.562 --> 00:02:31.690
towards 2021.

00:02:41.157 --> 00:02:44.869
(Video) Zarifa Ghafari: 
In the name of Allah. May God protect us.

00:02:57.048 --> 00:03:02.011
Driver: I’m Mayor Zarifa Ghafari’s driver,
and I guard her 24/7.

00:03:03.346 --> 00:03:04.723
And I'm very happy

00:03:04.764 --> 00:03:06.850
to be serving a woman

00:03:07.726 --> 00:03:10.770
who so completely serves her country

00:03:10.812 --> 00:03:12.188
and her people.

00:03:21.656 --> 00:03:26.453
ZG: After we pass these deserts,
we’ll feel safer.

00:03:30.832 --> 00:03:34.377
Slow down. Drive slowly.

00:03:35.003 --> 00:03:36.713
Cars are heading towards us.

00:03:39.507 --> 00:03:41.134
(Horn blares)

00:03:45.472 --> 00:03:47.599
Driver: I drive above the speed limit,

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:51.060
one hand on the steering wheel,
one on my pistol,

00:03:51.102 --> 00:03:54.773
because the road
I drive Zarifa on every day

00:03:55.356 --> 00:03:58.568
between Kabul and Maidan Shar
is controlled by the Taliban.

00:04:04.657 --> 00:04:10.705
She is a champion for me and our people.

00:04:12.207 --> 00:04:16.419
I live every second of my life
with the memory of her.

00:04:25.386 --> 00:04:27.555
KC: So the film spans a period --

00:04:27.597 --> 00:04:30.975
I think you started
working on the project in 2020

00:04:31.017 --> 00:04:32.769
and Zarifa, the main character,

00:04:32.811 --> 00:04:37.524
is the youngest female mayor
in Afghanistan,

00:04:37.524 --> 00:04:39.234
in the capital city of a province

00:04:39.234 --> 00:04:43.530
that has always had
a strong Taliban presence.

00:04:43.530 --> 00:04:49.494
And you did go out and you filmed
in the mountains with the Taliban.

00:04:49.494 --> 00:04:52.956
You said earlier that one
of your favorite parts of filmmaking

00:04:52.997 --> 00:04:55.708
is talking with people
and telling their stories.

00:04:55.750 --> 00:05:00.713
It must have been difficult meeting people
whose views you don't share.

00:05:00.755 --> 00:05:04.175
Tell us a little bit about how you managed
to build those relationships

00:05:04.217 --> 00:05:07.679
and how you were able to film there.

00:05:08.930 --> 00:05:13.017
TA: I think filmmaking and storytelling
is more about listening to people,

00:05:13.059 --> 00:05:16.688
understanding them,
no matter how different they are.

00:05:16.688 --> 00:05:17.981
So this is how --

00:05:17.981 --> 00:05:22.777
When we started working
on this documentary, "In Her Hands,"

00:05:22.777 --> 00:05:24.112
that's how it started.

00:05:24.112 --> 00:05:27.532
So with Zarifa, for me, it was easier
because she was a woman,

00:05:27.532 --> 00:05:29.242
she was from my own generation

00:05:29.242 --> 00:05:32.620
and we were trying
to make a change in our communities,

00:05:32.620 --> 00:05:36.457
using different tools or different ways.

00:05:36.499 --> 00:05:41.004
But then also we thought that's important
to include also the Taliban,

00:05:41.045 --> 00:05:47.135
because there was peace talks going on
between the US government and the Taliban,

00:05:47.176 --> 00:05:48.928
but Afghans were not included.

00:05:48.970 --> 00:05:50.388
And we, as Afghan woman,

00:05:50.430 --> 00:05:54.267
knew that we will be
more affected than anyone

00:05:54.309 --> 00:05:56.686
if there is any change in the country,

00:05:56.728 --> 00:06:00.231
especially if Taliban
will come back to power.

00:06:00.231 --> 00:06:04.027
KC: When you met with some
of the senior leadership

00:06:04.027 --> 00:06:06.362
in the region of the Taliban,

00:06:06.362 --> 00:06:07.822
how did they respond to you?

00:06:07.822 --> 00:06:10.909
Were you able to have conversations

00:06:10.909 --> 00:06:12.410
and did their opinion of you,

00:06:12.410 --> 00:06:14.954
do you think, change
over the course of the filming?

00:06:15.997 --> 00:06:19.125
TA: When I first started
contacting the Taliban,

00:06:19.167 --> 00:06:21.669
so basically we did it
through the leadership.

00:06:21.711 --> 00:06:25.173
We contacted them to be able
to go there and film them.

00:06:25.214 --> 00:06:28.301
It took us some time, they did not
respond to messages on time.

00:06:28.343 --> 00:06:31.596
So it took us weeks and months
to get permission.

00:06:31.638 --> 00:06:34.349
But then once I was there,
because I was a woman,

00:06:34.390 --> 00:06:39.187
and then after --
they said after 25 or more years,

00:06:39.228 --> 00:06:43.733
there was an Afghan filmmaker,
a woman on the mountains with them.

00:06:43.733 --> 00:06:46.569
So they were quite shocked
to see me there.

00:06:46.569 --> 00:06:51.699
So when I was doing the first interview,
they tried to not look at me.

00:06:51.699 --> 00:06:55.453
But then I remember
by the time they started,

00:06:55.453 --> 00:06:57.372
you know, being more comfortable.

00:06:57.372 --> 00:07:00.541
And then at the end,
I remember when I was saying goodbye

00:07:00.583 --> 00:07:04.170
to one of the leaders,
one of the commanders,

00:07:04.212 --> 00:07:07.215
he was blushing and he wanted
to shake hands with me.

00:07:07.256 --> 00:07:10.218
And then that time I wasn't sure.
I was like, what will happen?

00:07:10.259 --> 00:07:14.514
Because I see all these other
Taliban commanders and Taliban soldiers,

00:07:14.555 --> 00:07:16.474
and they had their guns with them.

00:07:16.516 --> 00:07:21.104
So I was like, what if I shake hands
and something happens to me?

00:07:21.145 --> 00:07:26.067
And I remember our local producer
who visited Taliban so many times before,

00:07:26.067 --> 00:07:29.278
he kept saying, "Tamana,
be careful, don't do that."

00:07:29.278 --> 00:07:30.571
So, yeah.

00:07:30.571 --> 00:07:32.115
KC: And did you shake his hand?

00:07:33.032 --> 00:07:36.744
TA: He didn't want to do that at the end.
So yeah, we left at that.

00:07:36.744 --> 00:07:37.996
KC: Yeah.

00:07:41.708 --> 00:07:46.295
(Video) Solider 1: The government 
has failed the people of Afghanistan.

00:07:47.171 --> 00:07:51.718
Ask the people here whether the government
has done anything for them.

00:07:53.678 --> 00:07:56.389
Solider 2: We are from this country,
and we will serve it.

00:08:09.610 --> 00:08:11.487
Solider 3: Brave Mujahideen!

00:08:11.487 --> 00:08:16.617
Solider 4: Until we establish Islamic rule
across the whole planet,

00:08:16.617 --> 00:08:19.287
we will not surrender to anyone.

00:08:20.830 --> 00:08:23.166
I have been put here to fight people.

00:08:24.292 --> 00:08:27.211
I have been instructed to kill people.

00:08:27.253 --> 00:08:30.465
To destroy people, behead people.

00:08:31.132 --> 00:08:33.801
Until they believe
there is no god but Allah.

00:08:41.267 --> 00:08:45.730
Musafer: I’ve fought in these mountains 
since the US invasion in 2001.

00:08:47.940 --> 00:08:52.945
Look at me, I am 36 years old,

00:08:52.945 --> 00:08:58.284
but everyone thinks
I am 40 or 50 years old.

00:09:02.622 --> 00:09:08.628
KC: This is with the same people who were
sending written death threats to Zarifa,

00:09:08.669 --> 00:09:10.213
the main character in the film,

00:09:10.254 --> 00:09:12.799
as early as 2017.

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:18.805
Tell us a bit about the risks
that both she and you faced

00:09:18.846 --> 00:09:23.309
working in a province that had
such a strong Taliban presence.

00:09:24.685 --> 00:09:27.105
TA: It just came back.
I can still feel it.

00:09:28.856 --> 00:09:31.359
It was not an easy experience

00:09:31.359 --> 00:09:36.239
because you know that any moment
you might get killed.

00:09:36.239 --> 00:09:39.033
And there were times
when we filmed at night,

00:09:39.033 --> 00:09:40.993
days and nights in Maidan Wardak,

00:09:40.993 --> 00:09:42.286
I was with her

00:09:42.286 --> 00:09:46.624
and there was fighting going on
between [the] Afghan army and the Taliban.

00:09:46.666 --> 00:09:48.501
And also, whenever we were in the car,

00:09:48.543 --> 00:09:52.004
I remember she used to
hold my hand really tight,

00:09:52.046 --> 00:09:56.676
saying that “Tamana, what if there is
a sticky bomb in this side of the car

00:09:56.717 --> 00:09:58.594
or that side of the car?”

00:09:59.762 --> 00:10:03.391
But, you know, when you're going
through so many crises,

00:10:03.432 --> 00:10:05.434
you find ways to deal with it.

00:10:06.602 --> 00:10:09.814
And then we try to remember
the good things

00:10:09.814 --> 00:10:11.524
about, you know, being there.

00:10:11.524 --> 00:10:15.403
And then I used to say that,
"Zarifa, we are very close to Kabul."

00:10:15.403 --> 00:10:19.657
It happened most of the time on the way
between Maidan Wardak and Kabul.

00:10:19.657 --> 00:10:22.326
Or I remember when we were in the city

00:10:22.326 --> 00:10:26.831
and we lost a few friends
in Taliban sticky bombs explosions.

00:10:26.873 --> 00:10:28.541
And we were down most of the time,

00:10:28.583 --> 00:10:31.002
but that doesn’t mean
that there was no life.

00:10:31.043 --> 00:10:32.795
We found ways to deal with it.

00:10:32.837 --> 00:10:34.338
We found ways to be happy.

00:10:34.380 --> 00:10:38.509
We found ways to find solutions,
at least to raise our voice.

00:10:38.551 --> 00:10:40.303
I remember we did campaigns.

00:10:40.344 --> 00:10:42.722
We did protests to --

00:10:44.765 --> 00:10:48.436
make people, our leaders,
and the world leaders,

00:10:48.477 --> 00:10:53.274
listen to what was happening
and what was waiting for us in the future.

00:10:53.274 --> 00:10:59.655
But unfortunately, I think we ended up
where none of us were ready.

00:11:01.365 --> 00:11:04.327
(Video) ZG: Whenever I meet people
from different communities,

00:11:04.744 --> 00:11:08.414
I ask them to educate their children

00:11:08.456 --> 00:11:13.753
because education is the only way out
of this situation in Afghanistan.

00:11:18.883 --> 00:11:21.761
Hello, how are you? Are you well?

00:11:21.886 --> 00:11:23.846
We thank you for coming here.

00:11:25.431 --> 00:11:26.557
Thank you.

00:11:30.603 --> 00:11:34.065
Our girls do study. There...

00:11:34.065 --> 00:11:36.984
There are some classes.

00:11:43.241 --> 00:11:49.121
ZG: As a girl, I studied.
I have a bachelor’s and a master’s degree.

00:11:49.121 --> 00:11:55.962
When I was studying, I had to walk
an hour each way to my school in Paktika.

00:11:56.003 --> 00:11:59.298
There were many
suicide attacks and fights.

00:11:59.882 --> 00:12:03.261
My father told me not to go anymore.

00:12:03.302 --> 00:12:05.721
So I started going to school secretly.

00:12:05.763 --> 00:12:08.933
I was going to school in secret
after he went to the office.

00:12:09.600 --> 00:12:14.105
It was my lifetime dream

00:12:14.855 --> 00:12:18.484
to see that my parents are proud

00:12:18.609 --> 00:12:22.280
of my reputation and my achievements.

00:12:23.239 --> 00:12:26.701
So please educate your daughters
and send them to school.

00:12:26.701 --> 00:12:32.581
If you educate one girl or woman,
you save ten generations.

00:12:34.333 --> 00:12:38.671
KC: You spoke about
how many women in Afghanistan

00:12:38.713 --> 00:12:41.507
were actively campaigning

00:12:41.549 --> 00:12:47.138
to continue to move forward
and not to fall back into old ways.

00:12:48.014 --> 00:12:54.312
As the filming progressed and you realized
that the Taliban were gaining power

00:12:54.353 --> 00:12:58.524
and more likely to come back
to a position of strength,

00:12:58.524 --> 00:13:00.693
how did that feel for you and Zarifa?

00:13:01.444 --> 00:13:05.281
Did it change when you were
in villages and cities?

00:13:05.281 --> 00:13:08.451
Could you tell there was
something different in the air?

00:13:08.451 --> 00:13:10.036
TA: Whenever I was in Kabul,

00:13:10.036 --> 00:13:12.788
I was not able to see the change

00:13:12.830 --> 00:13:14.707
because everything was OK.

00:13:14.749 --> 00:13:16.000
Everything was alright.

00:13:16.042 --> 00:13:20.087
But whenever I went to the villages,
to other provinces ...

00:13:20.129 --> 00:13:23.424
And I remember coming back,
I came with a lot of information,

00:13:23.466 --> 00:13:26.719
but I was not really able
to tell even my own family.

00:13:26.761 --> 00:13:29.930
I didn't want my sisters
to be scared of the future.

00:13:29.972 --> 00:13:34.310
I didn't want my mother to be scared
of her future and my future.

00:13:34.352 --> 00:13:36.103
And if I have told them that,

00:13:36.145 --> 00:13:39.231
OK, I saw that these things
were happening in the provinces,

00:13:39.231 --> 00:13:40.691
especially meeting Taliban,

00:13:40.691 --> 00:13:42.526
I knew that they were progressing.

00:13:42.526 --> 00:13:46.989
And I remember whenever I went
to other provinces in the past,

00:13:46.989 --> 00:13:48.574
I wasn't really sure about it

00:13:48.574 --> 00:13:51.535
but it was eye-opening
to see all, you know,

00:13:52.620 --> 00:13:55.039
the Taliban, people like me,

00:13:55.790 --> 00:13:57.458
ordinary Afghans,

00:13:57.500 --> 00:14:01.837
all of us, we were trying
to build a country in our own ways,

00:14:01.879 --> 00:14:04.298
but ended up destroying the whole country.

00:14:05.424 --> 00:14:06.675
And the --

00:14:07.927 --> 00:14:12.973
The flags changed when we crossed
from one territory to another territory.

00:14:13.015 --> 00:14:16.268
There was a republic flag,
and then there was [a] Taliban flag.

00:14:16.310 --> 00:14:18.562
So we knew that there is a difference.

00:14:18.562 --> 00:14:22.983
And I remember, Zarifa and I,
and other friends, female friends,

00:14:22.983 --> 00:14:26.487
we talked about it a lot.

00:14:26.487 --> 00:14:30.032
We were like, we need to push for it.
We need to do something to change it.

00:14:30.699 --> 00:14:36.914
We knew, we had the fear in our heart
that Taliban might come back,

00:14:36.956 --> 00:14:39.500
but we thought they will be
part of the government.

00:14:41.293 --> 00:14:42.878
And the way it happened --

00:14:42.920 --> 00:14:44.588
when the government collapsed,

00:14:44.630 --> 00:14:47.133
it was not only the government, it was --

00:14:48.509 --> 00:14:52.930
the people, full of hopes and dreams.

00:14:52.972 --> 00:14:58.394
And the future collapsed in front of us
and we couldn't do anything about it.

00:14:58.436 --> 00:15:02.565
We did have fear, but we didn't know
that it would end up the way it did.

00:15:03.941 --> 00:15:06.444
KC: And eventually it got so bad

00:15:06.444 --> 00:15:09.363
that Zarifa had to leave, she left.

00:15:09.363 --> 00:15:11.574
She came back. She had to leave again.

00:15:11.574 --> 00:15:13.868
You have had to leave.

00:15:13.868 --> 00:15:19.165
And it's something that very few people
have to make those difficult decisions.

00:15:19.206 --> 00:15:21.792
It must have taken a lot.

00:15:23.043 --> 00:15:26.005
Do you plan to go back if you can?

00:15:26.046 --> 00:15:27.339
Would you return?

00:15:30.676 --> 00:15:34.013
TA: After Toronto for one week
my heart was really calm

00:15:34.054 --> 00:15:35.848
and I was thinking about it,

00:15:35.890 --> 00:15:40.853
because since I left Afghanistan,
the fire was there, you know?

00:15:40.895 --> 00:15:45.483
And when I left, I left my soul
in Afghanistan with my loved ones,

00:15:45.483 --> 00:15:48.777
with my country, with my homeland,

00:15:48.777 --> 00:15:52.656
with everything I had and built
for all these years.

00:15:54.033 --> 00:15:58.829
The way we left,
we didn’t know that it [would] happen.

00:15:58.829 --> 00:16:03.125
And I remember when I left,
I did not say bye to my loved ones.

00:16:03.167 --> 00:16:06.295
I still remember my family.

00:16:06.337 --> 00:16:08.756
I still remember my friends.

00:16:10.007 --> 00:16:12.384
I told them that, you know,
this is not a goodbye.

00:16:12.426 --> 00:16:13.677
I cannot say goodbye.

00:16:13.719 --> 00:16:15.304
You don't know what will happen.

00:16:15.304 --> 00:16:16.805
I will come back.

00:16:16.805 --> 00:16:18.057
I will go to the airport.

00:16:18.057 --> 00:16:20.017
I don’t know [if] it will happen or not.

00:16:20.017 --> 00:16:21.268
I will be back.

00:16:21.268 --> 00:16:24.438
And I was making excuses because
I didn't want to leave my country.

00:16:24.438 --> 00:16:26.482
That was home. That was everything.

00:16:27.858 --> 00:16:30.736
Yeah, I kept my passport.

00:16:32.530 --> 00:16:35.449
But yeah, that's what keeps me alive.

00:16:37.076 --> 00:16:38.994
And people might have different dreams.

00:16:38.994 --> 00:16:41.914
Some people might think that,
OK, I want to travel.

00:16:41.956 --> 00:16:45.501
I want to make the best fun,
I want to do this and that.

00:16:45.543 --> 00:16:48.295
But my biggest dream
is to go back to Afghanistan

00:16:48.337 --> 00:16:50.506
and live the way I used to live.

00:16:51.924 --> 00:16:55.177
KC: We all hope that that will happen
sooner rather than later.

00:16:55.219 --> 00:17:01.350
But for now, we know that the situation
is very difficult for many women.

00:17:03.310 --> 00:17:05.437
You came to Europe

00:17:06.772 --> 00:17:11.068
and even here we've spoken
about the everyday sexism

00:17:11.068 --> 00:17:12.403
that you experience

00:17:12.403 --> 00:17:16.282
and how exhausting it is to educate men

00:17:16.282 --> 00:17:19.493
on issues around gender equality.

00:17:21.453 --> 00:17:22.788
Are there parallels?

00:17:22.788 --> 00:17:27.084
Do you see any opportunities
for us to learn from each other?

00:17:27.126 --> 00:17:30.045
Because I know many people
in the audience will feel your pain

00:17:30.087 --> 00:17:32.548
and recognize that exhaustion.

00:17:32.590 --> 00:17:34.133
How do we learn from each other?

00:17:34.174 --> 00:17:39.722
How do we work together
as women globally to create change?

00:17:39.763 --> 00:17:41.223
TA: Sexism is everywhere

00:17:41.265 --> 00:17:44.602
and we need to come together
to work on it.

00:17:46.395 --> 00:17:50.441
But the only thing I would say [is]
that it’s tiring and exhausting,

00:17:50.441 --> 00:17:52.526
but we should not give up. We cannot stop.

00:17:52.526 --> 00:17:54.028
KC: Absolutely.

00:17:54.028 --> 00:17:57.531
TA: So we need to have solidarity,

00:17:57.531 --> 00:17:59.992
to work on it together, to push for it.

00:18:00.784 --> 00:18:03.078
KC: And hearing stories
like yours, like Zarifa,

00:18:03.078 --> 00:18:06.206
there's an amazing line in the documentary

00:18:06.248 --> 00:18:09.627
where we follow her

00:18:09.668 --> 00:18:14.173
in such extraordinarily
difficult circumstances

00:18:14.214 --> 00:18:17.343
and she's constantly under threat.

00:18:17.384 --> 00:18:20.763
And she says, "Well, men have had
40-50 years to get this right

00:18:20.804 --> 00:18:22.514
and they haven't yet done it right.

00:18:22.556 --> 00:18:24.016
So I'm not going to give up."

00:18:24.058 --> 00:18:26.977
Is that representative
of the spirits of many women

00:18:27.019 --> 00:18:28.937
that you encountered in Afghanistan?

00:18:29.855 --> 00:18:33.400
TA: The spirit of almost
all the women I know in my country,

00:18:33.400 --> 00:18:37.321
including my grandmother,
my mother, my friends.

00:18:38.322 --> 00:18:40.157
All of us.

00:18:40.157 --> 00:18:42.326
Because, yeah, war is ugly.

00:18:42.326 --> 00:18:45.579
And it was the men who created the war.

00:18:45.579 --> 00:18:47.581
It was the men who made peace.

00:18:47.623 --> 00:18:49.249
We always pushed for it.

00:18:49.291 --> 00:18:52.795
But then there was no space
for us [at] the table,

00:18:52.836 --> 00:18:55.047
no matter how hard we pushed for it.

00:18:55.089 --> 00:18:59.718
But it doesn't mean
that we give up and it's done.

00:18:59.760 --> 00:19:02.888
And we are, you know ...
We made peace with that.

00:19:02.930 --> 00:19:04.807
It was the spirit of the whole country.

00:19:04.848 --> 00:19:06.100
It still is.

00:19:06.141 --> 00:19:09.728
And it is the women who are [the]
resistance in Afghanistan today.

00:19:11.271 --> 00:19:15.192
They were protesting in solidarity
with Iranian women in Kabul,

00:19:15.192 --> 00:19:16.819
in front of [the] Iranian embassy.

00:19:18.987 --> 00:19:22.866
So yeah, I think the future is female.

00:19:22.866 --> 00:19:24.660
It's us.

00:19:24.660 --> 00:19:27.621
And we are the ones who can make change
because we don't fight.

00:19:28.747 --> 00:19:30.374
We don't care about ego.

00:19:30.416 --> 00:19:31.917
We care about solutions.

00:19:33.001 --> 00:19:36.296
KC: So all of the people listening today,
inspired by your story,

00:19:36.338 --> 00:19:38.507
by the strength of women in Afghanistan,

00:19:40.050 --> 00:19:42.386
what can we do to support the struggle?

00:19:42.428 --> 00:19:46.223
You said solidarity is key to change,
and I couldn't agree more.

00:19:46.265 --> 00:19:48.225
How do we work together?

00:19:48.267 --> 00:19:54.606
What can we do to keep the plight
of women in Afghanistan in the news

00:19:54.606 --> 00:19:58.026
and to make sure
that one day you're able to return

00:19:58.026 --> 00:20:00.320
and do the wonderful work
that you've been doing?

00:20:02.030 --> 00:20:06.285
TA: I think women in Afghanistan need
solidarity more than anything right now.

00:20:07.745 --> 00:20:10.539
I remember I was interviewing
women who were protesting

00:20:10.581 --> 00:20:14.501
because I was working on research
for Amnesty International.

00:20:14.543 --> 00:20:15.961
And whenever we talked,

00:20:16.003 --> 00:20:18.714
I was asking them
why they started protesting

00:20:18.756 --> 00:20:23.051
and they said that,
"Tamana, we lost everything we had.

00:20:23.093 --> 00:20:25.137
We are not able to work.

00:20:25.179 --> 00:20:29.016
We are not able to go out
without a mahram, a man, family member,

00:20:29.057 --> 00:20:32.227
and we are not able to move freely,

00:20:32.269 --> 00:20:35.981
travel to other provinces,
to countries outside Afghanistan

00:20:35.981 --> 00:20:37.274
without a man."

00:20:37.274 --> 00:20:43.447
So it means that Taliban do not count
women as humans or equal to men.

00:20:43.447 --> 00:20:47.159
So there should be a man
to validate that they exist.

00:20:48.076 --> 00:20:50.412
And they say that,
"We lost everything we had.

00:20:50.412 --> 00:20:52.039
And that's why we are fighting.

00:20:52.080 --> 00:20:54.708
We are not afraid to die,

00:20:54.750 --> 00:20:56.877
but it makes us happy

00:20:57.961 --> 00:20:59.379
and encourages us

00:20:59.421 --> 00:21:03.050
when we see women,
no matter Afghans, non-Afghans

00:21:03.091 --> 00:21:04.593
from outside the country,

00:21:06.094 --> 00:21:07.596
supporting us.

00:21:07.638 --> 00:21:08.889
And that's all we need."

00:21:08.931 --> 00:21:11.934
And they were thanking me all the time
for doing the interviews.

00:21:11.934 --> 00:21:15.187
And they were like, "You listen to us
and it's important for us."

00:21:16.605 --> 00:21:22.778
So there is a need to come together
and support the woman in Afghanistan,

00:21:22.778 --> 00:21:25.989
in Iran, or any part of the world,

00:21:25.989 --> 00:21:30.494
because all we fight for
is our basic human rights.

00:21:31.286 --> 00:21:34.373
I think it's really important
to support the woman in Afghanistan

00:21:34.373 --> 00:21:38.669
or any other part of the world
who are fighting for their basic rights.

00:21:38.710 --> 00:21:40.379
It's 2022.

00:21:41.421 --> 00:21:44.132
I know that women,
we as women, we are political.

00:21:44.174 --> 00:21:45.467
Our bodies are political.

00:21:45.509 --> 00:21:49.012
Our opinions, whatever we say,
whatever we do is political.

00:21:49.054 --> 00:21:53.475
Women get criticized more than men,

00:21:53.517 --> 00:21:55.644
and no matter in which field.

00:21:55.686 --> 00:21:58.814
And it's really important for all of us,

00:21:58.814 --> 00:22:00.607
no matter where in the world,

00:22:00.607 --> 00:22:05.112
to support those women
protesting on the streets in Kabul

00:22:05.112 --> 00:22:07.281
and other provinces of Afghanistan.

00:22:07.281 --> 00:22:09.575
And one of the ways we can do this,

00:22:09.575 --> 00:22:12.202
we can open a conversation
about Afghanistan,

00:22:12.202 --> 00:22:13.912
so it's on the news again.

00:22:13.912 --> 00:22:15.497
So people talk about it

00:22:15.539 --> 00:22:18.375
and we don't forget
what's happening there.

00:22:18.417 --> 00:22:22.004
I know that I was the lucky one
who is here now, who is able to work.

00:22:22.045 --> 00:22:24.840
But I also know
that it was painful to leave.

00:22:27.801 --> 00:22:30.262
But for them, because they are fighting,

00:22:30.304 --> 00:22:32.973
they need our support, solidarity.

00:22:33.015 --> 00:22:35.434
And we need to put pressure
on our governments,

00:22:36.393 --> 00:22:38.437
no matter where in the world,

00:22:38.478 --> 00:22:40.063
to change the policies,

00:22:40.063 --> 00:22:42.566
to put pressure on the Taliban,

00:22:42.566 --> 00:22:45.277
to change their policies
and make a change.

00:22:46.486 --> 00:22:50.616
KC: I think people can start
by watching "In Her Hands."

00:22:50.616 --> 00:22:54.536
It really is a beautiful film.

00:22:54.536 --> 00:22:57.372
It's incredibly compelling.

00:22:57.414 --> 00:23:00.751
And for me I found
there were threads of hope in there.

00:23:01.501 --> 00:23:04.963
So I think watching the film,

00:23:05.005 --> 00:23:09.635
understanding this incredible strength
of the women of Afghanistan,

00:23:09.676 --> 00:23:11.970
will inspire many, many people.

00:23:12.012 --> 00:23:15.724
And hopefully they will step up
and show that solidarity

00:23:15.766 --> 00:23:18.769
and continue to raise
awareness of the issue.

00:23:19.728 --> 00:23:23.065
You no doubt will continue to do so.

00:23:23.065 --> 00:23:27.611
I know you have more films, I think,
that you're hoping to make in the future.

00:23:29.029 --> 00:23:30.906
And we're just very, very lucky

00:23:30.906 --> 00:23:35.160
to have you, such a wonderful
and compelling storyteller,

00:23:35.160 --> 00:23:38.163
to shine a light on this issue

00:23:38.163 --> 00:23:41.875
and educate us all
so that we can stand in solidarity.

00:23:41.917 --> 00:23:43.168
So thank you.

00:23:43.210 --> 00:23:45.629
It's been wonderful
listening to you today.

00:23:45.671 --> 00:23:46.922
Thank you, Tamana.

00:23:46.964 --> 00:23:48.215
TA: Thank you.

